WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.015
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.015 --> 00:00:04.695
Maya wants help understanding a tricky homework question.

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:13.260
Her first thought is to paste an entire private conversation into a study tool, even though only one sentence explains the assignment.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:21.825
As you watch, keep one question in mind: Share the least information needed, follow the assignment, and keep control of your own work.

00:00:21.825 --> 00:00:24.680
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:32.040
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.234
Think of sharing information like packing for a short trip.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:41.546
Bring what you need for the task, not a whole drawer of personal things just in case.

00:00:41.546 --> 00:00:45.005
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:51.737
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:51.737 --> 00:00:54.763
Remove unnecessary personal details from a task.

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:58.222
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:00:58.772 --> 00:01:02.788
A friend offers to paste their draft into the chat to get feedback.

00:01:02.788 --> 00:01:09.382
Even if that seems convenient, the friend should decide what to share and remove details that are not needed.

00:01:09.382 --> 00:01:10.581
Pause for a moment.

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:13.998
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:13.998 --> 00:01:17.775
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:17.775 --> 00:01:19.813
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:25.148
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:25.698 --> 00:01:27.625
The assignment rules matter too.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:36.960
A teacher may allow help brainstorming but not allow generated paragraphs in the final submission, so Maya checks the instructions before choosing a tool.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.213
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:45.934
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:49.909
Distinguish permitted help from doing the assignment for someone.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.523
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:53.523 --> 00:01:57.498
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:58.048 --> 00:02:07.484
Before sending anything, ask whose information it is, whether the task permits the tool, and whether a smaller or anonymous example would work.

00:02:07.484 --> 00:02:10.123
If the answer is unclear, stop and ask.

00:02:10.123 --> 00:02:14.676
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:18.437
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:18.437 --> 00:02:24.640
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:30.787
What if the tool asks for a full name or a personal story to answer a basic study question?

00:02:30.787 --> 00:02:34.846
Look for a way to remove that detail or use a different resource.

00:02:34.846 --> 00:02:38.721
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:38.721 --> 00:02:45.486
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:45.486 --> 00:02:47.700
Write a specific AI-use disclosure.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.190
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:03:00.932
Instead of pasting a classmate's message, describe the assignment in general terms and ask for a checklist of questions to consider.

00:03:00.932 --> 00:03:04.717
Maya writes the response herself and notes any allowed help.

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.703
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:06.703 --> 00:03:09.496
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:09.496 --> 00:03:14.771
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:14.771 --> 00:03:17.750
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.404
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:22.954 --> 00:03:27.343
Now open the practice activity, Permission, privacy, or proceed?

00:03:27.343 --> 00:03:28.783
, when you are ready.

00:03:28.783 --> 00:03:34.405
In the privacy activity, decide whether each detail is truly needed for the task.

00:03:34.405 --> 00:03:38.520
Permission, school rules, and data minimization all matter.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.331
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:41.331 --> 00:03:45.720
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:49.834
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:50.384 --> 00:03:53.956
Your lesson project is called Repair an unsafe study request.

00:03:53.956 --> 00:04:00.105
For your project, list what information the tool needs and what it should never ask students to provide.

00:04:00.105 --> 00:04:02.682
Say how a student can choose not to use it.

00:04:02.682 --> 00:04:07.132
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:07.132 --> 00:04:08.889
What would they need to know?

00:04:08.889 --> 00:04:12.051
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:15.272
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:20.905
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:20.905 --> 00:04:23.637
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:23.637 --> 00:04:27.132
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:30.944
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:30.944 --> 00:04:36.726
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:44.714
Here is one idea to take with you: Share the least information needed, follow the assignment, and keep control of your own work.

00:04:44.714 --> 00:04:49.537
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:49.537 --> 00:04:53.895
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:53.895 --> 00:04:58.660
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.663
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:05:01.663 --> 00:05:07.647
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:07.647 --> 00:05:13.571
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:13.571 --> 00:05:19.195
Share the least information needed, follow the assignment, and keep control of your own work.

00:05:19.195 --> 00:05:21.529
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:25.418
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
